WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.773 --> 00:00:04.707
This is one polyester thread.

2
00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:09.893
In the next three minutes, we'll follow it from oil to soil.

3
00:00:09.893 --> 00:00:11.384
Or will we?

4
00:00:11.384 --> 00:00:17.259
By the end, you'll be able to explain why most polyester never comes back.

5
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.547
Polyester starts as oil and gas.

6
00:00:21.547 --> 00:00:30.277
Two chemicals, terephthalic acid and ethylene glycol, link up again and again into one long chain: PET.

7
00:00:30.277 --> 00:00:33.505
Each link gives off a little water.

8
00:00:33.505 --> 00:00:37.003
It was invented in Lancashire in 1941.

9
00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:42.921
Today, polyester is 59 per cent of all the fibre the world makes.

10
00:00:42.921 --> 00:00:48.481
And 88 per cent of that polyester comes from fossil fuels.

11
00:00:49.569 --> 00:00:55.440
The chain is melted, pushed through tiny holes, and stretched into filament.

12
00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.235
Here, designers make choices.

13
00:00:58.235 --> 00:01:03.066
Cut it short and spin it, and it can feel like cotton.

14
00:01:03.066 --> 00:01:03.982
Twist it.

15
00:01:03.982 --> 00:01:04.782
Ply it.

16
00:01:03.982 --> 00:01:06.149
Wrap it in another fibre.

17
00:01:06.149 --> 00:01:15.197
Dye it, and it fights back: polyester repels water, so its dyes only get in at about 130 degrees, under pressure.

18
00:01:15.197 --> 00:01:17.975
Every choice changes how it feels.

19
00:01:17.975 --> 00:01:21.953
And every choice decides what can happen to it later.

20
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.316
This thread is wrapped in cotton.

21
00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:27.120
Will the recycler take it?

22
00:01:28.272 --> 00:01:29.419
It jams.

23
00:01:29.419 --> 00:01:35.023
Blends are hard to separate, so most blended clothing can't go round again.

24
00:01:35.023 --> 00:01:39.208
Most recycled polyester doesn't come from clothes at all.

25
00:01:39.208 --> 00:01:41.161
It comes from plastic bottles.

26
00:01:41.161 --> 00:01:48.189
Worldwide, less than one per cent of the material used to make clothing is recycled into new clothing.

27
00:01:49.598 --> 00:01:51.972
So what if we bury it?

28
00:01:51.972 --> 00:01:56.662
In a compost test, cotton fabric broke down within months.

29
00:01:56.662 --> 00:01:58.967
The polyester stayed intact.

30
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:06.276
In sunlight, polyester becomes brittle and cracks into smaller and smaller pieces.

31
00:02:06.276 --> 00:02:08.261
It breaks up, not down.

32
00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:09.806
So: oil to soil?

33
00:02:09.806 --> 00:02:11.288
Not polyester.

34
00:02:11.288 --> 00:02:12.311
Not yet.

35
00:02:13.359 --> 00:02:15.286
Three quick questions.

36
00:02:15.286 --> 00:02:17.375
What is PET made from?

37
00:02:17.375 --> 00:02:20.180
Why does a blend jam the recycler?

38
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.619
Where does most recycled polyester come from?

39
00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:25.938
Now, one question for you.

40
00:02:25.938 --> 00:02:30.908
What would you design, if every garment had to come back to you?

41
00:02:30.908 --> 00:02:32.092
Try the lab, and find out.

